\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A complex-type real plane with a square determinant}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{On the real Euclidean plane take the orthogonal complex
structure and the rotation
##{\mathcal J=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 R=-\tfrac{1}{2}\bbI +\tfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\mathcal J.}
The matrix #{R} has order three and no real eigenline,
so defines an irreducible real #{C_3} representation.
A matrix commutes with #{R} exactly when it commutes with
#{\mathcal J}. Solving that equation gives
#{a\bbI +b\mathcal J}, with #{a,b\in\mathbb R}, so the
commutant is #{\mathbb C}, in the sense of
\ref{fdip-00VC}.}

\p{The form #{b(x,y)=\langle x,\mathcal Jy\rangle}
is alternating and perfect: #{\mathcal J^{\mathsf T}=-\mathcal J}
and #{\mathcal J^2=-\bbI}. It is invariant because the
orthogonal action commutes with #{\mathcal J}. Its inverse
transport adjoints agree with the positive Euclidean ones.
The degree-pair cancellation in \ref{fdip-00V6}, which uses
only alternation and the raising/lowering adjoint relation,
therefore applies to its cochains. The criterion
\ref{fdip-00UX} gives a nonnegative square determinant.}

\p{For the one-vertex circle with link #{R}, put
#{A=R^{-1}-\bbI}. In degree order #{0,1},
##{K=\begin{pmatrix}m\bbI&A^{\mathsf T}\\A&m\bbI\end{pmatrix}.}
Since #{R+R^{-1}=-\bbI},
#{A^{\mathsf T}A=2\bbI -R-R^{-1}=3\bbI}. For nonzero
#{m}, block elimination gives
#{\det K=\det(m^2\bbI -A^{\mathsf T}A)=(m^2-3)^2}.
Both sides are polynomials in #{m}, so this identity also
holds at zero. Thus a real complex-type commutant can coexist
with an alternating form and a determinant-square constraint.
This real plane differs from the complex character line in
\ref{fdip-00UM}.}
